// legend.templ — the `legend` layer's section, under the month
// (C-CALV4-LAYERS-P9, W-F).
//
// This zone carried the signed `needs backend` chip for two waves, and the
// reason was honest: "every mark's axis LABEL ('Quest', 'Festival') is not in
// BlockData — Mark carries a hue, a pattern and a glyph, but no axis name — so
// a legend built here would be invented." r52 added Mark.AxisLabel for the
// Ledger's meta line and said in terms that it "incidentally unblocks W-F's
// legend". This is that unblocking, and it needed no new pin field.
//
// The chip retires the way the wave-1 chips were designed to: the data arrived,
// so the zone renders content instead. Nothing about the zone's placement,
// class or geometry changed.

package calendar_block

// legendZone renders one swatch per axis value with its viewer-filtered count.
//
// THE COUNTS COME FROM ONE PASS over the same Rows[].Cells[].Marks the grid
// drew (buildLegend), which is why the legend, the grid and the docked Ledger
// cannot disagree: there is exactly one place a mark lives. It joins the count
// oracle for the same reason — the sum of these counts must equal the viewer's
// own visible mark total on the signed GM/Nissa/Bryn fixture, or the number is
// decoration.
//
// TYPE AXIS ONLY. There is no Mark.OwnerLabel and no per-calendar label, so the
// signed picker's other two options cannot be built; they are booked to
// C-CALV4-AXIS-P11 rather than stubbed. A stub here would be a control that
// promises a grouping the data cannot produce.
//
// AN EMPTY LEGEND RENDERS NOTHING AT ALL — no zone, no heading, no "0 types".
// A month with no marks the viewer can see has no legend, and an empty box
// saying so is worse than absence: absence is already the product's vocabulary
// for "there is nothing here".
//
// IT OPENS RATHER THAN SITS OPEN (C-CALV4-TILES §9.4). What changed is ONLY the
// disclosure: the entries, their swatches, their counts and the single pass that
// builds them are untouched, so the count oracle keeps asserting the same bytes
// (`<label> <span class="n">N</span>`) it always did. A legend that rebuilt its
// content on the way to becoming a disclosure would have been two changes
// wearing one name.
//
// THE MECHANISM IS A CLIPPED CHECKBOX PLUS ITS LABEL, which is the idiom the
// moon panel, the day pick, the tie toggle and the Shelf's tabs already use —
// and it is not a preference. boot.js:163 sets `htmx.config.allowScriptTags =
// false`, so a <script> inside an HTMX-swapped fragment never executes; a JS
// disclosure would work until the first binding swap and then silently stop, in
// production only.
//
// THE CHECKBOX IS WHAT MAKES IT REACHABLE BY TOUCH. Hover alone strands a phone
// — there is no pointer to rest on the tab — and the stylesheet's hover branch
// is gated on `(hover: hover)` for that reason. A tap lands on the label, the
// label checks the box, and the box holds the zone open until it is pressed
// again. The keyboard lands on the box itself, which is why it is CLIPPED
// rather than `display: none`: a display-none input is not focusable.
//
// THE COPY IS A COLOUR KEY AND NOTHING ELSE. §9.2 made the rune decorative — the
// glyph is chosen by :nth-child(), i.e. by position in the day — so no shape
// carries a type and nothing here may imply one does. legendTabLabel() states
// the axis the swatch actually carries.
templ legendZone(d BlockData) {
	if entries := buildLegend(d); len(entries) > 0 {
		<div class="layer" data-layer="legend">
			<div class="legend">
				// NAMED BY ITS OWN LABEL, so no aria-label is set: the <label>
				// is associated by `for`, and an aria-label would override the
				// visible text with a second copy of itself — two names for one
				// control is how the two come to disagree.
				<input type="checkbox" class="legpin vhctl" id={ legendPinID(d) }/>
				<label class="legtab" for={ legendPinID(d) }>{ legendTabLabel() }</label>
				<div class="legbody">
					for _, e := range entries {
						<i>
							<span class={ legendSwatchClass(e) } style={ legendSwatchStyle(e) } aria-hidden="true"></span>
							{ e.Label }
							<span class="n">{ intText(e.Count) }</span>
						</i>
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	}
}
